\par }\pard \ql \fi-540\li540\ri0\keep\widctlpar\tx180\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin540\itap0 {\fs22\cf1\insrsid6764735 \tab A.  I wonder why the dog's wound doesn't become infected.
\par \tab B.  The dog's saliva must prevent the growth of infection-causing bacteria.
\par \tab C.  I'll obtain a bacterial culture and grow the same kind of bacteria in two identical culture dishes. Once the bacteria start growing, I'll add dog saliva to only one of the dishes and lea
ve the other alone. I'll cover both dishes. Then I'll observe what happens each day for a week.
\par \tab D.  Even after adding the dog saliva to one of the dishes, the bacteria continued to grow in both dishes over the course of the week. However, the bacteria in the treated dish grew more slowly than the bacteria in the untreated dish.
\par \tab E.  I think I'll try something else. I'll start with two identical culture dishes, as before, and use the same kind of bacteria in each dish, but this time I'll treat one dish with
 dog saliva before I add the bacteria. I'll observe what happens each day for a week.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\keep\widctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs22\cf1\insrsid6764735 NARREND
